{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1791193280,"nonce":"/ko0e/Lgp4d0EyZqghHMtw1X6hI=","target":"7cabafe069d55b45fbf77b7e1cbefdf0eae0ceac4f7df22cc6b5bc3794ab8fb0","message":"Fluent methods should beat the stylesheet, with a component-default tier below app CSS\n\nQueued. Not started, and deliberately behind the open priority work (see\n\"Ordering\"). Evidence and the surrounding design are in 6f7b001; this is the\nfirst step of that plan, written so it can be picked up cold.\n\n\nWhen an element sets a property with a fluent method AND a stylesheet rule\nmatches it, the stylesheet wins. Observed with a throwaway test (removed):\nbg(red) with a class whose rule says background blue renders blue, and\nrounded(3) against border-radius 20px renders 20. The stylesheet pass runs\nafter the build and orders rules by specificity; nothing in it knows which\nfields the element set itself. The web does the opposite: inline style beats\nthe stylesheet. The wanted model is CSS-first, overridable by the element's\nown fluent calls.\n\n\nCSS winning is deliberate. cn_demo's theme bundle carries\n`#css-overrides .cn-button--primary { border-radius: 0; }`, which works\nbecause CSS beats the component's own fluent rounded(). cn sets visual\ndefaults fluently about 300 times (bg 91, rounded 75, border 42, gap 37,\npx 20, py 17, p 13, opacity 8). A plain flip makes every one of them beat\nthe application's CSS.\n\nSo three tiers, not two:\n\n    component default  \u003c  app stylesheet  \u003c  call-site fluent method\n\nEach property keeps a value PER TIER and resolves to the highest present.\nRemoving a stylesheet rule then reveals the component default underneath\ninstead of leaving nothing, which is the job the base_styles snapshot does\ntoday (and its documented failure is a snapshot contaminated with hover\nstate). Blinc already hand-rolls this per field: border_radius_explicit,\ncorner_shape_locked, incidental_align_self, explicit_color, in about 59\nplaces.\n\n\n1. A per-property tier in the node's props, written by one writer. Fluent\n   methods write Owned. The CSS pass writes Stylesheet. Component defaults\n   write Default.\n2. Component internals: base(|s| ...) on a refinement, or, as a migration\n   shim only, a scope guard that makes writes inside a component constructor\n   land in Default. Prefer base() for new code; the guard is implicit state.\n3. Move or wrap cn's roughly 300 fluent defaults. Mechanical, and findable\n   by grep.\n4. Only then change precedence.\n\n\nA test that renders a cn component under a user stylesheet and asserts the\ntheming path. It must pass BEFORE the change and AFTER it.\n\n- Build a cn button, apply `.cn-button--primary { border-radius: 0; }` as an\n  app stylesheet, and assert the resolved corner radius is 0.\n- The throwaway probe used RenderTree::from_element, set_stylesheet with\n  Stylesheet::parse, apply_stylesheet_layout_overrides,\n  apply_stylesheet_base_styles, then get_render_node(id).props to read the\n  resolved background and border_radius. That is enough to write the gate.\n- Add a second test for the new behaviour, #[ignore]d until it lands: an\n  element with rounded(3) and a class whose rule says 20px resolves to 3.\n- Add a third: removing the stylesheet rule restores the component default\n  rather than clearing the value.\n\n\nAn element holding an Owned value is not changed by a CSS :hover rule,\nbecause Owned outranks the stylesheet. That is the web's behaviour for inline\nstyles but will surprise people; such an element uses hover(..) instead.\n\n\n- Whether cn's stylesheet and an application's are told apart by origin or\n  only by order. A Default tier for component CSS needs an origin.\n- Whether the per-tier slots belong inside RenderProps or beside it.\n\n\nBehind the current priorities: what the ashui session has asked for, and the\nin-flight work on measurement, text and the rich text editor. The open items\namong those are 124ad5d (fallback-font advances in measurement), a62df59\n(taffy max-content collapse), 81183df (BC4 index quality), and the\nblinc_cn tree view and sheet design note. Nothing here blocks any of them.\n\n6c086bf and 4375436 carry priority:high in the tracker. That label was not\nreordered; this issue was queued behind the ashui work only.\n\nSteps 2 to 4 of the plan (generated Tailwind-style vocabulary, the refinement\ntype for variants, a tw! string macro) stay in 6f7b001 until this lands.","files":null}]}